---
title: Encryption model
description: How NeoWorks encrypts media end-to-end — the account master key, the cross-origin Vault, per-object envelopes, and sharing — and what is deliberately left queryable.
---

NeoWorks splits data into two classes and encrypts them differently:

- **Media** (files, images, video, documents — anything that is just bytes) is **end-to-end
  encrypted**. The server only ever holds ciphertext.
- **Queryable records** (contacts, calendar, tasks) are **not** end-to-end encrypted — they
  must be filtered, sorted and searched server-side. See [storage](/docs/neoworks/storage).

This page documents the media encryption design and the decisions behind it.

## Threat model & goals

- The server is **honest-but-curious for media**: it stores and serves blobs but must not be
  able to read them.
- Compromise of the database or object store must not expose plaintext media.
- An application embedding NeoWorks (calendar, photos, muse, …) must be able to encrypt and
  decrypt media **without ever holding the user's master key** — even a malicious or buggy app
  should not be able to exfiltrate it.

## The account master key (AMK)

Every account has a 32-byte **Account Master Key**. It is derived from the user's password via
Argon2id and wrapped (sealed) so it can be stored and recovered, but it is **never stored in
usable form** on the server and **never enters an application's memory**.

From the AMK, an X25519 **account keypair** is derived deterministically
(`BLAKE2b(AMK, "nw-account-keypair-v1")` → `crypto_box` seed). This keypair is what other users
seal data *to* when sharing, and what the owner uses to unseal their own data.

## The Vault: a cross-origin crypto sandbox

> **Decision: applications never touch the AMK.** All encryption and decryption happens inside
> a sandboxed **Vault** served by the OAuth origin and embedded by each app as an iframe.

The Vault is a page (`/vault` on the OAuth server) that an app loads in a cross-origin
`<iframe>`. The app talks to it only over `postMessage`:

- `encrypt(plaintext, wrappedDEK?)` → `{ ciphertext, wrappedDEK }`
- `decrypt(ciphertext, wrappedDEK)` → `plaintext`
- `rewrap(wrappedDEK, recipientPubKey)` → `wrappedDEK` (for sharing)

The AMK lives only inside the Vault's origin and **never crosses the iframe boundary**. The app
sends plaintext in and gets ciphertext out (or vice-versa); it never sees keys. Browser
**storage partitioning** keeps each embedder's Vault state isolated, and the Vault uses the
[Storage Access API](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/API/Storage_Access_API) to reach
its first-party session for authenticated key operations.

Which origins may embed the Vault is allowlisted server-side
(`frame-ancestors` CSP, configured by `VAULT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`). An origin not on the list
cannot frame the Vault at all.

## Per-object envelope encryption

Each media object gets its own random **data-encryption key (DEK)**:

1. A fresh 32-byte DEK is minted per object.
2. Content is encrypted with **AES-GCM** under the DEK. Output is `[12-byte IV ‖ ciphertext]`.
3. The DEK is **sealed to the account public key** with `crypto_box_seal` → the **wrapped DEK**
   (a base64 string). It is safe to store: useless without the account private key inside the
   Vault.

Large files are split into chunks; every chunk reuses the same per-object DEK (the first chunk
mints it, the rest pass the returned wrapped DEK back in).

### Content-addressed, deduplicated chunks

Ciphertext chunks are addressed by the **SHA-256 of the ciphertext**. Upload first asks which
chunk hashes are missing, then uploads only those. Deduplication is intentionally **per user** —
two users encrypting the same file produce different ciphertext, so chunks never collide across
accounts. Chunks are reference-counted and garbage-collected when no media (or version) points
at them.

## Sharing without re-encrypting

> **Decision: share by re-sealing the DEK, not by re-encrypting the bytes.**

To share an object, the owner's Vault unseals the DEK and **re-seals it to the recipient's
account public key** (`rewrap`). The DEK plaintext never leaves the Vault. The server stores:

- a **`media_grant`** row authorizing the recipient to read the object's chunks, and
- the recipient's **wrapped DEK** in the object's `recipients` list, keyed by their user id.

Each reader fetches the manifest and receives **their own** wrapped DEK. Revocation drops the
grant and the recipient's wrapper. (Revocation does not rotate the DEK, so content a recipient
already downloaded is not retroactively protected — a deliberate simplification.)

## Devices & recovery

The AMK is also wrapped to each of the user's **devices** (a per-device keypair stored in the
Vault's partitioned storage) so returning users unlock silently instead of re-entering a
password. Account recovery re-wraps the AMK to guardians' devices. Device registration is
authenticated by the OAuth session, which is why apps must be served on an origin that is
[same-site with the OAuth server](/docs/neoworks/devices).

## What is *not* encrypted

End-to-end encryption applies to **media bytes only**. Queryable data (contacts, calendar,
tasks) is stored so the server can index and filter it. Media **metadata** that powers listing
and search — filename, MIME type, size, timestamps — is likewise queryable, not E2E. When media
is made searchable by content, a further tradeoff applies: see
[searchable encrypted media](/docs/neoworks/encrypted-search).

> Don't describe NeoWorks as "everything is encrypted." The accurate claim is: **media is
> end-to-end encrypted; queryable data and media metadata are not.**
